speed.patch - the renderer's processor side made several times cheaper: the changes to files
other work is going on in.  docs/issues/native-render-speed.md says what it is and what was run.

Made against tools/recomp as it stood at the time in the line "snapshot" below.  Apply from
tools/recomp:

    cd tools/recomp && patch -p1 --dry-run < render/speed.patch && patch -p1 < render/speed.patch

The new files it needs are already in place and are not in the patch:
    render/vu1_translate.py  render/vu1_translate_gen.py   the translator of the VU1 microprograms (a tool)
    render/vu1_fast.h  render/vu1_simd.h                   what the translated programs are written against
    render/vu1_fast_none.cpp                               linked when there is no translation
    render/vu1_fast_test.cpp                               the four-wide arithmetic against the interpreter's
    render/prof.h                                          instruction counters per part (-DRTA_PROF only)
    render/speedbench.py                                   builds and runs decode_bench in the variants compared

After the patch, once (the output is derived from the game and stays under local/):

    python3 tools/recomp/render/vu1_translate.py --elf SLUS_209.73 \
        --stream local/recomp-speed/streams/fire3-fight.rtavs --stream local/recomp-speed/streams/hub.rtavs \
        --out local/recomp-out/gen-vu/vu1_fast.cpp --report
    python3 tools/recomp/app/build.py            # and render/build.py replay: both link gen-vu/vu1_fast.cpp when it is there

(--elf gives the main program; the two small programs the game loads behind it every frame come
from a recorded stream of a fight - any stream written by `app record FILE` in a level that uses
them.  A program that is not translated is interpreted, as before.  A translation that does not
match what is in microprogram memory is never used: it is compared by hash.)

Notes for the merge
 - Nothing changes unless gen-vu/vu1_fast.cpp exists, except four things that are on by default
   and give the same lists (proof in the document): Gs::triangle_run (a whole run of triangles in
   one loop), a kicked packet read where it lies, Vif1::unpack_fast (one loop per UNPACK format),
   and Gs::start_transfer's bookkeeping
   (the list of blocks a rectangle writes is kept; a table of the blocks upload sets start at).
 - Run-time switches, all in the environment, read once in Renderer's constructor (renderer.cpp):
     RTA_VU1_INTERPRET=1   every VU1 program interpreted (the reference)
     RTA_VU1_OLD=1         not the new translation; the older one of the main program if linked
     RTA_GS_SLOW=1         every vertex through packed() / kick(), kicked packets copied first
     RTA_VIF_SLOW=1        every UNPACK through the old function
   In code: Vu1::use_fast, Gs::fast_runs, Gs::copy_kicks, Vif1::fast_unpack.  rta_replay has
   --reference (all four), --interpret, --old-translation; decode_bench --interpret and
   --old-translation; web_main's --interpret-vu covers the new translation too.
 - render/gs.cpp (the file with the most traffic): one new function (triangle_run, with its
   macro) placed before emit_vertex; five lines in gif() after a tag is read; xgkick() split in
   two (the packet is measured first, then read where it lies or copied); start_transfer's last
   lines; `set_at[...]` beside the three places `sets` gains or loses an entry - if a later change
   adds another sets.erase() or sets[...], it needs the same line; the static `log_far` moved
   from inside emit_vertex to file scope; RTA_PROF_SCOPE lines, which are empty without -DRTA_PROF.
   Nothing in rebuild_state, kick, packed, texture_for or the texture code is changed.
 - render/vu1.cpp: decode() and the hash of the older translation are now made only when the
   interpreter is really needed and only for the pairs that changed (micro_changed(first, count);
   the old micro_changed() is "all of it"): Vif1's MPG passes the range.
 - render/renderer.h: the renderer's own VU1 memories are aligned to 16 bytes (a kicked packet
   is read in place as words).
 - app/build.py, app/build_win.py, render/build.py, web/build.py: one job more each
   (gen-vu/vu1_fast.cpp or render/vu1_fast_none.cpp - without it the programs do not link);
   web/build.py takes it as --vu-fast FILE, with --flag=-msimd128.  app/build_win.py had changed
   in the shared tree by 21:26; the hunk applies with an offset.  The Windows program was not
   built here.
 - web/decode_bench.cpp: counters, a checksum of the draw commands beside the one of the
   vertices, --no-check, --list FILE, --old-translation.
 - Windows: vu1_simd.h has SSE2 for x86-64 (tested here under Rosetta: vu1_fast_test.cpp) and
   falls back to plain C++ elsewhere; the flush-to-zero mode is used on AArch64 with clang or gcc
   only (MSVC on ARM64 gets NEON doing the masking itself).  The generated file needs the flags of
   vu1.cpp: no contraction of multiply-add, rounding-mode access (/fp:strict).
 - Found on the way, not changed (docs, section "Found on the way"): after rebuild_state() sends
   a texture again (restore_texture -> gif(3, ...)), cur_path stays 3 for the rest of the packet
   being read, so those triangles are marked made_by 1 and treated as 2-D by the layout.
